# Prior art
New or complex work starts from what already exists, so survey it before you design or write code.
Load the `prior-art` skill for the procedure: search online for similar products and open-source projects, check each candidate's licence and say plainly whether it can be used here, compare three to five real options with their trade-offs, and name the pick and the reasons.
Replace the survey line below with the record, then run `@ROOT@/bin/jg-prior-art-gate.sh @HOME@/data/tlo/brief.md`; it refuses the section left empty or unreplaced, and passes only with a recorded pick and its licence decision.
Prior art (survey): <replace with `Prior art pick: <name>` and `Licence: <licence - usable here or not>` lines, or with `Prior art: none needed - <why this work is neither new nor complex>`>

# Local trunk lane
This project is registered local-trunk: there is no feature pull request, and the only pull request is the release one, raised on the captain's word.
Your branch must be exactly ONE commit on top of the local trunk tip (the `trunk` branch of the project, which is the commit your worktree starts on; compare against that branch only, never an older trunk branch), and the commit message must carry its ticket reference (the task id or its ticket key).
Juggernaut's landing check rebases your commit onto the latest tip of the local trunk by itself, lints the changed files, runs the changed files' tests against the tip's own result and lands it; you never rebase for it. Your done and the landing both need the whole proof at your exact head: a PASS completion receipt, approved merge-card evidence that cites files, and a sealed done-done run holding them; a PASS receipt alone is refused, and nothing may be committed after the seal. The owner signature is recorded after the landing and never blocks it.
If the landing check reports that your change conflicts with the trunk, YOU resolve every conflict in your own change, then report ready again; never hand a conflict to anyone else, and never force a landing: no force flag, no hand-moved trunk ref, no landing past a conflict exists, and a landing that is refused is fixed in the lane and reported ready again.
A stale or conflicting change is refused with its paths named and is never landed dirty.
Commit, run the QA step of the Definition of done, and report ready with your done line. Juggernaut's landing check then lands the commit on the trunk and cleans up unforced; you never land, push, go live or release.

